WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.840
INTRO:
CEOs and founder CEOs are by far the richest

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.529
people in the world.

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:09.960
While there are some oddballs on the billionaires
list who are directors, investors, and even

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.690
athletes, the vast majority are company leaders.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.020
Unlike most jobs though, company leaders are
rarely paid in cash.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.880
Many of the top CEOs take a cash salary of
just $1 per year.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.640
And even for the CEO’s that take a moderate
cash salary, the cash portion is an extremely

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.240
small portion of their total compensation.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.620
For example, out of Sundar Pichai’s $281
million salary in 2019, only $2 million was

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.199
paid in cash.

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:41.210
Considering this, how do billionaires even
access the extraordinary wealth they have

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.760
without crashing their’s company’s stock.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.920
And what about founder CEOs that own 40 or
50% of a massive conglomerate.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.729
For example, Bernard Arnault owns 47% of LVMH
which gives him a mind boggling net worth

00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:57.729
of $123 billion.

00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:00.210
But is it even possible for him to cash out?

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.940
Well, at his age, the answer is probably not,
but here’s how billionaires access their

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.490
paper fortunes and how they live a rich lifestyle
without cash.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:15.380
TIME-BASED STOCK VESTING:
The first thing to note about most of these

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.590
billionaires' compensation structure is that
they can’t cash out their salary even if

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.869
they wanted to.

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:25.810
This is especially applicable to non-founder
CEOs who don’t already have a lot of equity.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:30.469
You see, the last thing board members want
is for a CEO who either quits or is fired

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:34.079
under negative circumstances to dump all of
their shares and crash the stock on their

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:35.079
way out.

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:39.219
So, a restriction they implement to protect
against this is stock vesting.

00:01:39.219 --> 00:01:42.460
The first type of stock vesting is time-based
stock vesting.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.649
Time-based stock vesting is usually most common
amongst non-founder CEOs.

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:50.800
And as the name suggests, these CEO’s are
restricted from selling any of their stock

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.429
compensation for a set period of time.

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:57.270
This rule doesn’t just apple to executives
either, employees at these companies are generally

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.320
subject to the similar restrictions.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.490
Both Google and Apple for instance follow
a 4 year vesting schedule with a 1 year vesting

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.109
cliff for employees.

00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:09.190
The vesting cliff simply means that you can’t
sell any of your stock within the first 1

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:13.210
year, and after that you’ll get access to
your stock on a linear basis over the next

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.210
4 years.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.610
So, really, you wont get access to all of
your stock till 5 years after your given that

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:19.820
compensation.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.640
And if you leave during this time period,
you of course lose access to your remaining

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.640
stock.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.410
CEO’s follow a similar structure, but their
vesting periods are way larger given that

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.630
their compensation is also way larger.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:36.660
For example, when Tim Cook was hired as CEO
in 2011, they gave him 1 million shares of

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.840
Apple, but the stock vesting period was 10
years.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.530
The first half million shares would unlock
in August of 2016 and the other half million

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.950
would unlock in August of 2021.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:51.420
This is why we saw insane headlines about
Tim Cook receiving $750 million worth of stock

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.420
last year.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.310
While this is technically true, it’s also
kind of misleading.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:01.030
When Tim was awarded these shares in 2011,
they were worth $384 million.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.740
And given a vesting period of 10 years, this
basically translates to $38.4 million per

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:12.060
year which is still an insane amount, but
it’s no where close to $750 million in a

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.340
single year.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:17.140
Tim actually went ahead and sold this entire
compensation right after he got it.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.470
But, thanks to the vesting period, Apple stock
had grown to a point at which it could easily

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.940
sustain this selling pressure and protect
shareholder value.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.510
MILESTONE-BASED STOCK VESTING:
The other most common type of stock vesting

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.380
is milestone based vesting.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.060
Instead of tying up stock access to a time
period, this option ties up stock access to

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.500
various milestones.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.770
This type of vesting is most common amongst
founder CEOs who already have loads of equity.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.360
Given that they already have plenty of shares,
it’s not the board members’ top concern

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.680
to get these CEOs even more invested in the
stock.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.910
For example, Bernard Arnault already owns
47% of LVMH.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:59.890
So, pumping up his stake 55 or 60% probably
wouldn’t motivate him that much more.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:03.910
As a result, board members are able to be
much more strict with their compensation plans,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.590
requiring these CEO’s to not only put up
time but put up results.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.160
A great example of milestone based vesting
is Elon Musk and Tesla.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.850
Elon Musk receives no salary, no bonus, and
no time-based equity.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:21.090
The only way for him to earn more stock is
to reach the milestones outlined in this compensation

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.090
plan.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:26.910
As you can see, this plan consists of both
market cap milestones and operational milestones,

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.220
and he must hit both of these numbers to be
compensated.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.400
When these milestones were established in
2018, Tesla’s marketcap was just $59 billion,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.180
so reaching a $650 billion market cap was
quite an ambitious goal.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:45.140
But Elon has since crushed these numbers which
has given him paydays worth tens of billions.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.510
It should also be noted though that if he
didn’t reach these ambitious milestones

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:52.860
within 10 years, he would’ve gotten no compensation
for 10 years of work.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.520
This is obviously rather harsh, so many companies
tend to go for a more softened approach.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.850
They’ll often combine time based vesting
with milestone based vesting to create a hybrid

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.130
vesting model.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.350
This way, CEO’s don’t have to achieve
insane growth numbers to be paid, but at the

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.639
same time, they can’t just sandbag their
time at the top either.

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:15.010
For example, this was one of the caveats that
Apple introduced to Tim’s compensation plan

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.490
in 2013.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:21.950
They clarified that Apple must also beat the
S&amp;P 500 for Tim to receive his full compensation.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:25.930
For a company like Apple, beating the S&amp;P
500 throughout the 2010s definitely wasn’t

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:31.170
that challenging of a goal, but it did introduce
some level of accountability.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:36.610
DEBT:
Alright, so companies use time and milestone

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:41.470
based vesting to keep executives loyal to
the company over the long term, but what about

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:42.470
during the meantime?

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.650
For example, from Tim Cook’s perspective,
it’s great that he could receive up to 1

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:51.780
million Apple shares in 10 years, but how
can he enjoy the benefits of that right now?

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:57.330
This is obviously a rich person problem, but
the solution is rather interesting to discuss.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.620
The answer to this problem is usually debt.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:05.320
While Tim couldn’t sell most of the stock
he received in 2011 he could use it as collateral.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:10.110
Now, trying to collateralize RSU income or
restricted stock income is not the easiest

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.800
of tasks for us normal people.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.380
But, for company leaders, this is rather easy
and it’s not just because they’re in a

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:17.800
powerful position.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:21.960
The main reason is that the items that they’re
trying to buy are usually a small fraction

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.340
of their income.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.680
For example, Tim recently bought a $9.1 million
house.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.900
I have a feeling that he just paid cash for
this property given that he bought this house

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.880
right after his stock compensation unlocked.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.580
But, it wouldn’t have been that hard for
him to take out a mortgage either given that

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:39.520
the house costs less than a 1/30th of his
compensation plan.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.870
Similarly, Elon took out $61 million worth
of mortgages in 2019 which was likely a large

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:49.630
amount compared to his cash reserves but a
miniscule portion compared to his net worth.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.030
This reasoning obviously doesn’t apply to
the average person who’s buying a house

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.260
that’s worth 2 to 5 times their gross income.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:00.280
For billionaires though, this strategy doesn’t
just apply to housing, it applies to basically

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:04.260
every facet of their life whether they’re
buying a private jet or Twitter.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:08.010
Also, this strategy isn’t just to work around
stock vesting periods either.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.919
There’s loads of benefits to leveraging
debt from maximizing returns and minimizing

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:13.750
taxes to benefiting from inflation.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:20.240
So, these stock vesting periods are just even
more reason to take on debt for this billionaires.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.370
LOOSE CANNONS:
Everything we’ve covered so far accounts

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.240
for the vast majority of CEOs.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.620
It’s usually just a case of borrowing against
your RSUs to fund your lifestyle until you

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.870
unlock your compensation packages.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:37.140
There are a few dozen people in the world,
however, who have significantly more power.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:41.400
For example, no one can prevent Jeff Bezos,
Bill Gates, or Elon Musk from selling their

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.850
initial stakes in the company.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.520
So, technically, these guys could try to sell
all of their stock in one go and crash the

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.520
market.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:51.970
But, given that these guys are founders, it’s
extremely rare that they make such a decision,

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.510
but it does happen from time to time.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:58.050
And I don’t think you’d be surprised to
hear that one of the CEOs that was bold enough

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.590
to do this was Steve Jobs.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:04.830
In 1985, Steve Jobs got entangled in months
of vicious battles with the CEO at the time

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.050
John Sculley.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:10.640
And as you would guess, this resulted in him
eventually being ousted in September of 1985.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:16.520
Out of rage, Steve would go ahead and sell
his entire 11% stake in Apple except for 1

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:17.520
share.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.449
Looking back at Apple stock during the time,
it doesn’t actually look like Apple fell

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:22.430
that much when Steve actually sold.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.900
Rather, most of the sell off took place leading
up to Steve being fired.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:30.790
By the time Steve departed the company, the
stock was down a painful 77% from their all

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:31.820
time high.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:36.550
So, while Steve selling his entire stake was
a monumental event, his sale itself didn’t

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:40.360
impact the stock too much given that most
of the fear was already priced in.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.579
Another example of a founder selling out is
Travis Kalanick and Uber.

00:08:44.579 --> 00:08:49.069
Like Steve Jobs, Travis was ousted from Uber
after months of boardroom battles in December

00:08:49.069 --> 00:08:50.570
of 2019.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.459
And he of course sold all of his stock right
after he was ousted.

00:08:53.459 --> 00:08:57.889
What’s interesting though is that he also
sold near the bottom just like Steve Jobs.

00:08:57.889 --> 00:09:03.389
Now, Uber is at even lower levels today, but
that’s besides the point.

00:09:03.389 --> 00:09:07.480
The point is that by the time a founder sells
all of their shares in a rage fit, the stock

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:12.230
is already in the toilet, so their selling
itself doesn’t hurt it all that much.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:15.769
If they were to just randomly sell out of
the blue though, the stock would no doubt

00:09:15.769 --> 00:09:17.750
get obliterated.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:23.399
CASHING OUT:
Aside from loose cannon examples, most founder

00:09:23.399 --> 00:09:26.139
CEOs cash out extremely strategically.

00:09:26.139 --> 00:09:30.629
For example, Jeff Bezos sells a few billion
worth of stock every year, but he doesn’t

00:09:30.629 --> 00:09:34.589
do so without months of warning and he sells
it small digestible batches.

00:09:34.589 --> 00:09:38.350
So, the stock price is rarely affected by
his sales.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:42.320
Likely the closest example to randomly selling
out of the blue is Elon’s recent sale for

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:43.320
Twitter.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:49.370
In late April, Elon Musk sold $8.5 billion
worth of Tesla stock or about 1% of the company.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:53.540
This led to Tesla stock selling off about
10% by the time he finished selling.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:57.759
This means that selling 1% of the company
correlates to about a 10% selloff.

00:09:57.759 --> 00:10:01.931
So, if a founder was to sell 10% out of the
blue, that would likely cause the stock to

00:10:01.931 --> 00:10:04.459
crash 70 or 80%.

00:10:04.459 --> 00:10:07.889
And that’s not even taking into account
that the sell off will accelerate as the founder

00:10:07.889 --> 00:10:09.410
continues to sell.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:13.149
The only way for a founder to sell out of
such a position is to spend decades slowly

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:15.589
selling their stock just like Bill Gates did.

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:20.120
When Microsoft went public in 1986, Bill Gates
owned 45% of Microsoft.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.759
Today, he owns just 1.34% of the company.

00:10:23.759 --> 00:10:28.490
Now, this puny 1.34% stake still correlates
to a massive $25 billion.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:32.129
But, given that Bill Gates has over a hundred
billion dollars, the vast majority of his

00:10:32.129 --> 00:10:34.589
wealth is no longer in Microsoft.

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:37.569
This makes him one of the most liquid billionaires
in the world.

00:10:37.569 --> 00:10:41.139
But, to get to this position, it took him
36 years.

00:10:41.139 --> 00:10:45.890
And, if any other mega cap founder wants to
cash out without destroying the stock, this

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:49.009
is really the only option they have as well.

00:10:49.009 --> 00:10:51.970
And that’s how CEO’s cash out of their
massive positions.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:55.870
If you were the founder of a mega cap company,
would you hodl your stake to your grave or

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.730
would you slowly sell off like Bill?

